#: content/docs/background.md:0
msgid "History & Architecture"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:13
msgid "History"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:14
msgid ""
"KDE Linux was conceived by a cabal of core KDE contributors in the offices "
"of [Tuxedo Computers](https://www.tuxedocomputers.com) during the early 2023 "
"Plasma sprint in Augsburg, Germany. One of them — veteran KDE developer "
"Harald Sitter — built and began refining a prototype, revealing it publicly "
"during Akademy 2024 ([slides](https://conf.kde.org/event/6/contributions/202/"
"attachments/135/171/The%20Operating%20System.pdf), [recording](http://www."
"youtube.com/live/gTxRaBEUe-I?t=25936))."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:16
msgid ""
"An [alpha release](https://pointieststick.com/2025/09/06/announcing-the-"
"alpha-release-of-kde-linux/) followed at Akademy 2025 ([slides](https://conf."
"kde.org/event/9/contributions/265/attachments/171/220/growth.pdf), "
"[recording](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=PA03J1CAYoA))."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:19
msgid "Reason for existence"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:20
msgid ""
"Be “the KDE operating system” so KDE can have direct relationships with "
"hardware vendors and users"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:21
msgid "Showcase KDE software in its most positive light"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:22
msgid ""
"Offer a high-quality UX competitive with or exceeding that of proprietary "
"alternatives"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:23
msgid ""
"Don’t make users manually install device drivers, optional KDE features, etc."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:24
msgid "Be highly reliable, and easy to recover"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:25
msgid ""
"Improve KDE software deployed using containerized apps and image-based "
"operating systems in general"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:26
msgid ""
"Any edition can be used as the main system by our developers for internal "
"dogfooding purposes"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:29
msgid "Things we have consciously decided not to care about right now"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:30
msgid ""
"Proprietary NVIDIA kernel driver for graphics cards older than NVIDIA GTX "
"16xx (newer ones use the open-source kernel modules, which are included)."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:31
msgid ""
"VirtualBox - requires out-of-tree kernel modules, and other VM solutions "
"work fine."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:32
msgid "Vendor-specific VPNs - require custom out-of-tree kernel modules"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:36
msgid "Architecture"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:37
msgid "Design"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:38
msgid ""
"KDE Linux incorporates lessons learned from the earlier [KDE neon](https://"
"neon.kde.org/) project, and focuses on resilience, recoverability, user-"
"friendliness, and low maintenance needs. The design is heavily inspired by "
"systemd creator Lennart Poettering’s thoughts on [operating system (OS) "
"design](https://0pointer.net/blog/fitting-everything-together.html) and "
"[system extensions](https://0pointer.net/blog/testing-my-system-code-in-usr-"
"without-modifying-usr.html)."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:40
msgid ""
"As such, KDE Linux is an “image-based” OS. This means the entire base OS is "
"provided as one unit, rather than being split up into thousands of packages "
"that are assembled into the final product on your computer."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:42
msgid ""
"Sometimes this model is called “immutable” because the base OS cannot be "
"modified in the same way a traditional OS made of packages could. However, "
"this terminology can be misleading; in KDE Linux, only <tt>/usr</tt> is "
"provided in the form of a read-only image, and everything else on the system "
"is modifiable like on any other Linux-based OS. And [<tt>/usr</tt> is "
"modifiable through the use of systemd extensions](override-usr)."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:45
msgid "High-level implementation"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:46
msgid "systemd-boot is used as the bootloader."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:48
msgid ""
"The bootable image is an ISO-compatible <code>.raw</code> disk image built "
"using <code>mkosi</code>. The base system comes from Arch Linux packages, "
"with most KDE content built using <code>kde-builder</code>. Some KDE apps "
"and 3rd-party apps are pre-installed Flatpaks."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:50
msgid ""
"<tt>/</tt> is a read-write Btrfs volume. <tt>/usr</tt> is mounted on top of "
"it in the form of an EROFS image/filesystem. Updating the system entails "
"downloading a new one of these and its companion unified kernel image (UKI). "
"3-4 of these OS images and their UKIs are cached on disk at any given time, "
"providing the ability to roll back in case of problems after updating."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:52
msgid ""
"A small helper program updates <tt>/etc</tt> from the content in <tt>/usr/"
"share/factory/etc</tt> in the active OS image for anything you haven’t "
"changed yourself. Btrfs snapshots of <tt>/etc</tt> are generated "
"automatically."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:54
msgid "<tt>/home</tt> is a Btrfs subvolume."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:56
msgid ""
"User-facing apps shown in Discover come from [Flathub](https://flathub.org)."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/background.md:58
msgid ""
"No package manager is included, but [multiple methods](more-software) are "
"available for installing software that’s not available as a Flatpak app. "
"This is an evolving topic that’s likely to change over time."
msgstr ""

