#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:0
msgid "Switch Between KDE Linux and Another Operating System"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:11
msgid ""
"If you have KDE Linux installed alongside another Linux-based operating "
"system, you can normally reach both of them through your computer’s firmware "
"boot menu — the one you get by pressing a key such as <kbd>F12</kbd> while "
"the machine starts up."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:13
msgid ""
"That works, but it means catching a key press within a second or two on "
"every restart. This page describes how to add an entry for each system to "
"the *other* system’s boot menu, so you can switch between them from a menu "
"that waits for you."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:15
msgid ""
"{{< section class=\"text-center warning-bubble\" >}} This page does **not** "
"explain how to install two operating systems side by side. It assumes you "
"already have both installed and working, both installed in UEFI mode, and "
"sharing the same EFI System Partition (ESP). Note that [installing KDE Linux "
"to anything other than an entire disk](install/#4-explore-the-live-session-"
"and-install-kde-linux) is known to fail under certain circumstances and is "
"not a formally supported configuration yet. {{< /section >}}"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:20
msgid "Which system am I typing this on?"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:21
msgid ""
"Each section below is labelled with the system its commands belong on, "
"because the two are not interchangeable:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:23
msgid ""
"**On KDE Linux**, administrator commands use <code>run0</code>, as "
"everywhere else in this documentation."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:24
msgid ""
"**On the other system**, they most likely use <code>sudo</code> instead, and "
"file locations vary by distribution."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:26
msgid "Running a command on the wrong system will at best do nothing."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:29
msgid "If the other system also uses systemd-boot"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:30
msgid ""
"There is usually nothing to set up. systemd-boot finds entries by looking at "
"the ESP, so if both systems write to the same one, both should already "
"appear in the same menu."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:32
msgid "To check, run the following **on KDE Linux or the other system**:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:34
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"bootctl list\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:39
msgid "If the other system uses GRUB"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:40
msgid ""
"GRUB and systemd-boot do not see each other automatically, so an entry has "
"to be added on each side."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:42
msgid "Add KDE Linux to GRUB’s menu"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:43
msgid "*Do this **on the other system**, the one that uses GRUB.*"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:45
msgid ""
"Add the following to <code>/etc/grub.d/40_custom</code>, below the lines "
"that are already there:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:47
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"menuentry \"systemd-boot\" --class systemd {\n"
"    insmod part_gpt\n"
"    insmod fat\n"
"    insmod chain\n"
"    search --no-floppy --file --set=root /EFI/systemd/systemd-bootx64.efi\n"
"    chainloader /EFI/systemd/systemd-bootx64.efi\n"
"}\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:57
msgid ""
"Nothing in there needs to be adjusted: <code>bootctl</code> always installs "
"systemd-boot at that same path, whichever distribution it was run on."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:59
msgid "Then make the file executable and regenerate GRUB’s configuration:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:61
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"sudo chmod +x /etc/grub.d/40_custom\n"
"sudo grub-mkconfig -o /boot/grub/grub.cfg\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:66
msgid ""
"Some distributions name these differently — for example <code>grub2-"
"mkconfig</code> and <code>/boot/grub2/grub.cfg</code> on Fedora and "
"openSUSE. Check what your distribution uses."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:68
msgid "Add GRUB to KDE Linux’s menu"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:69
msgid "*Do this **on KDE Linux**.*"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:71
msgid ""
"For this direction you first need to know where GRUB lives on the ESP. There "
"is no single answer: every distribution picks its own directory name there, "
"so this has to be looked up rather than copied."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:73
msgid "To find it:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:75
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"run0 find /boot -name 'grub*.efi' 2>/dev/null\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:79
msgid ""
"Take the result and drop everything before <code>/EFI</code>. If you get "
"<code>/boot/EFI/debian/grubx64.efi</code>, the path you want is <code>/EFI/"
"debian/grubx64.efi</code>."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:81
msgid ""
"If Secure Boot is enabled, look in that same directory for <code>shimx64."
"efi</code> and use that path instead, if there is one. Not every "
"distribution ships one."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:83
msgid ""
"Now open a text editor, create a file called <code>grub.conf</code> in your "
"home directory, and put this in it:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:85
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"title    GRUB ([name for the other OS goes here])\n"
"efi      [path to the EFI file goes here, e.g. /EFI/debian/grubx64.efi]\n"
"sort-key zz-grub\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:91
msgid ""
"Replace the part in the first line with whatever you would like the entry to "
"be called in the menu, and the part in the second line with the path you "
"just looked up. Then move the file into place:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:93
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"run0 mv ~/grub.conf /boot/loader/entries/grub.conf\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:97
msgid ""
"<code>bootctl list</code> should now show it, and it will appear at the "
"bottom of the boot menu on the next restart."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:100
#, fuzzy
#| msgid "GeoNotes"
msgid "Notes"
msgstr "GeoNotes"

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:101
msgid ""
"If each menu’s default entry is the *other* bootloader, give at least one a "
"non-zero timeout, or you will bounce between them like in a particle "
"accelerator. So avoid doing that."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:102
msgid ""
"A shared ESP holds kernels for both systems, so it can fill up. <code>df -h /"
"boot</code> is worth a look if updates start failing."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:103
msgid ""
"Updates on either system can change the firmware boot order or remove "
"entries. If something changes unexpectedly, <code>efibootmgr -v</code> and "
"<code>bootctl list</code> are good places to start looking."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/dual-boot.md:104
msgid ""
"If the two systems have separate ESPs, the GRUB side may still work, but the "
"systemd-boot side generally will not, since it can only load files from its "
"own ESP."
msgstr ""

