#: content/docs/openqa.md:0
msgid "openQA for Developers and Maintainers"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:11
msgid ""
"KDE Linux uses [openQA](https://open.qa/) to automatically test every system "
"image before it is published. openQA boots each image in a virtual machine "
"and checks that it can be installed, upgraded, and used successfully."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:13
msgid ""
"openQA has some specific terminology: a *job* is one execution of a test "
"suite against an image, while a *worker* is the machine or container that "
"runs the job. The [openQA documentation](https://open.qa/docs/) explains "
"these and other concepts, including test modules, job groups, assets, and "
"the web UI."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:15
msgid "How the tests work"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:17
msgid ""
"The test worker runs alongside the CI pipeline, so it can use the images and "
"virtual disks produced by the build without uploading those large files "
"elsewhere. Tests are supplied to the virtual machine through a system "
"extension and communicate with it over SSH."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:19
msgid ""
"Desktop tests use the accessibility API through `selenium-webdriver-at-spi`, "
"allowing them to interact with applications and check their state without "
"relying solely on screenshot matching. The suite also checks for failed "
"system services, crashed desktop processes, networking problems, and "
"regressions in KDE Linux commands."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:21
msgid "CI pipeline and maintainer workflow"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:23
msgid ""
"On the protected default branch of the upstream [KDE Linux repository]"
"(https://invent.kde.org/kde-linux/kde-linux), the pipeline works as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:25
msgid ""
"The *imaging* job builds, signs, and stages the image and its sysupdate "
"channel on `storage.kde.org`."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:26
msgid ""
"The *trigger-openqa* job starts the pipeline in [os-autoinst-distri-kdelinux]"
"(https://invent.kde.org/kde-linux/os-autoinst-distri-kdelinux), passing the "
"staged image URL and sysupdate channel URL to it."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:27
msgid ""
"The downstream pipeline runs the normal install-and-sanity flow and the "
"upgrade flow."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:28
msgid ""
"The upstream *publish* job runs only after the openQA pipeline has "
"succeeded. It promotes the already-staged image for public consumption."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:30
msgid ""
"This makes an openQA failure a gate for image publish. Start by opening the "
"downstream pipeline from the `trigger-openqa` job, then open the failing "
"openQA job from its CI log. The job page shows the test modules in order and "
"lets you identify whether the failure is in installation, the installed-"
"system sanity tests, or the upgrade path."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:32
msgid "Download a staged image"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:34
msgid ""
"The link provided after the log message *“In case of failure, you can "
"inspect and download the .iso image and sysupdate tree at…”* opens a storage "
"browser for the build under test. Download the `.iso` there to boot it "
"manually or give it to a local openQA stack. The `sysupdate` tree contains "
"the staged update artifacts used by the upgrade test."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:36
msgid "Investigate a failed job"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:38
msgid ""
"Open the job in the openQA web UI and select the failed module first. The "
"link to the web UI will appear after the *“<name> test job is now running.”* "
"log message. Its details include the module result, screenshots or video "
"where applicable, and diagnostic output. In the *Logs & Assets* tab, "
"download or view `autoinst-log.txt` for the openQA worker and test-engine "
"log. This is the primary log for the job itself."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:40
msgid ""
"For tests that execute commands on the system under test, KDE Linux runs "
"them in transient systemd services and records the service journal as "
"diagnostic output in the test result. Inspect `autoinst-log.txt` for the "
"command output and journal. You can also download the `*kde-linux-collected-"
"logs.tar.zst` file for logs captured by the `collect-logs` tool."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:42
msgid "Run tests locally"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:44
msgid ""
"You can use openQA to test a locally built image before submitting a change. "
"This is required when working from a fork outside `kde-linux/kde-linux` - "
"its CI pipeline builds a test image, but does not trigger the openQA "
"pipeline. It is also useful while developing tests for your own changes."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:46
msgid ""
"The [KDE Linux openQA test repository](https://invent.kde.org/kde-linux/os-"
"autoinst-distri-kdelinux) includes a local stack containing both an openQA "
"web UI and a worker. It requires Podman and `podman-compose`, which are "
"included in KDE Linux."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:48
msgid ""
"Clone the test repository, then place the KDE Linux `.iso` image you want to "
"test in its root directory. If no image is present, the test runner "
"downloads the latest publicly available image instead."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:50
msgid "Start the local stack."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:52
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"./mock.sh up\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:56
msgid ""
"Once it is ready, open `http://localhost:1080` to inspect the openQA web UI. "
"Jobs are not submitted automatically, so, open a shell in the container and "
"submit the normal install and sanity-test flow."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:58
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"podman exec -it openqa-single-instance bash\n"
"./qa flow\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:63
msgid "To run the upgrade flow instead, add <code>--upgrade</code>:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:65
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"./qa flow --upgrade\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:69
msgid ""
"To run the disk-encryption install-test flow, add <code>--encrypt</code>:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:71
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"./qa flow --encrypt\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:75
msgid ""
"You can also combine <code>--upgrade</code> and <code>--encrypt</code> "
"options:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:77
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"./qa flow --upgrade --encrypt\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:81
msgid ""
"The `sanity-test` job needs the `install-system` job to run first because it "
"uses the virtual disk created by the installation. To concentrate on a "
"particular test, adjust the submitted jobs in `lib/worker/job_flow.py` or "
"run an individual job with <code>./qa job</code> while keeping that "
"dependency in mind. Run <code>./qa job --help</code> to see the options it "
"requires."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:83
msgid ""
"For example, to run an `install-system` job where you have an `.iso` "
"downloaded to the directory of the repository:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:85
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"./qa job \\\n"
"    --live ./kde-linux_&lt;build-id&gt;.iso \\\n"
"    --hdd kde-linux_&lt;build-id&gt;.qcow2 \\\n"
"    --sysext ./openqa-sysext.img \\\n"
"    --build &lt;build-id&gt; \\\n"
"    --name install-system \\\n"
"    --flavor live\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:95
msgid "When you are finished, stop the stack and remove its local volumes."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:97
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"./mock.sh down -v\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:101
#, fuzzy
#| msgid "Minetest"
msgid "Write a test"
msgstr "Minetest"

#: content/docs/openqa.md:103
msgid ""
"The test definitions and the test code live in [os-autoinst-distri-kdelinux]"
"(https://invent.kde.org/kde-linux/os-autoinst-distri-kdelinux). Add the test "
"to the appropriate flow in `main.pm` - the live-install flow, installed-"
"system sanity flow, or upgrade flow. Tests are generally composed of a small "
"openQA wrapper in `tests/` and a Python test that runs on the system under "
"test from `extensions/openqa/usr/lib/kde-linux-openqa/tests/`."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:105
msgid "Choose the test type based on what you are checking:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:107
msgid ""
"Use a normal Python <code>unittest</code> for command-line tools, services, "
"filesystem state, or other non-graphical behaviour."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:108
msgid ""
"Use Selenium through `selenium-webdriver-at-spi` when the behaviour requires "
"interacting with a graphical application."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:110
msgid "Normal Python tests"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:112
msgid ""
"Create a <code>unittest</code> in the aforementioned system extension test "
"directory. It should make assertions about the system under test and write "
"its results to a JUnit XML file, which will be automatically collected as an "
"openQA asset and a GitLab CI report."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:114
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"import unittest\n"
"from lib.sut import openqa_junit_xml\n"
"\n"
"class ExampleTests(unittest.TestCase):\n"
"    def test_expected_behaviour(self):\n"
"        self.assertTrue(True)\n"
"\n"
"if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n"
"    openqa_junit_xml.run(ExampleTests, \"example\")\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:126
msgid ""
"Create a matching wrapper under `tests/` that gets openQA to execute the "
"test on the host."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:128
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"from testapi import *\n"
"from lib.test import cli_test\n"
"\n"
"def run(self):\n"
"    cli_test.CliTest(\"example\").run_python()\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:136
msgid ""
"Finally, register the wrapper in the relevant flow in `main.pm`. The wrapper "
"collects the JUnit result and command output so that failures appear in the "
"openQA job and CI artifacts."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:138
msgid "Graphical tests with Selenium"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:140
msgid ""
"Graphical tests are also Python <code>unittest</code> classes, but use the "
"Appium/Selenium driver to find and interact with accessible UI elements. The "
"Selenium runner enables the accessibility infrastructure and starts the "
"driver for you."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:142
msgid ""
"For example, a graphical test creates a driver for its application, "
"interacts with accessible elements, and closes the driver afterwards:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:144
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"import unittest\n"
"from appium import webdriver\n"
"from appium.options.common.base import AppiumOptions\n"
"from appium.webdriver.common.appiumby import AppiumBy\n"
"from lib.test import openqa_junit_xml\n"
"\n"
"class ExampleTests(unittest.TestCase):\n"
"    @classmethod\n"
"    def setUpClass(cls):\n"
"        options = AppiumOptions()\n"
"        options.set_capability(\"app\", \"org.kde.example.desktop\")\n"
"        cls.driver = webdriver.Remote(\"http://127.0.0.1:4723\", "
"options=options)\n"
"\n"
"    @classmethod\n"
"    def tearDownClass(cls):\n"
"        cls.driver.quit()\n"
"\n"
"    def test_expected_behaviour(self):\n"
"        self.driver.find_element(AppiumBy.ACCESSIBILITY_ID, \"example-control"
"\").click()\n"
"\n"
"if __name__ == \"__main__\":\n"
"    openqa_junit_xml.run(ExampleTests, \"example\")\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:169
msgid ""
"Run the matching wrapper with <code>run_selenium()</code> instead of "
"<code>run_python()</code>. Pass the installed test user for desktop "
"applications:"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:171
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"from testapi import *\n"
"from lib.test import cli_test\n"
"from lib.common import user_manager\n"
"\n"
"def run(self):\n"
"    cli_test.CliTest(\"example\").run_selenium(user=user_manager."
"installed())\n"
"</pre>\n"
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:180
msgid ""
"Similarly to normal Python tests, add its wrapper to `main.pm`, run it "
"locally while developing it, and include it in the test flow that exercises "
"the feature."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:182
msgid ""
"For more information on writing Selenium tests, see [Appium automation "
"testing](https://develop.kde.org/docs/apps/tests/appium/) and [GUI Testing "
"with selenium-webdriver-at-spi](https://community.kde.org/Selenium)."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/openqa.md:184
#, fuzzy
#| msgid "Learn More"
msgid "Learn more"
msgstr "Lerni pli"

#: content/docs/openqa.md:186
msgid ""
"For a more detailed explanation of the test flow and its implementation, see "
"[openQA Testing in KDE Linux](https://blogs.kde.org/2026/07/13/openqa-"
"testing-in-kde-linux/). The test infrastructure is developed in the [os-"
"autoinst-distri-kdelinux repository](https://invent.kde.org/kde-linux/os-"
"autoinst-distri-kdelinux)."
msgstr ""

#: content/docs/override-usr.md:0
msgid "Add or Override Content in /usr"
